f that  well i can understand precisely what  she's saying i mean this is  disappointing right we need to go  further and faster but i would just say  within the confines of multilateralism  which is incredibly difficult very  frustrating nobody gets what they want  at the end of the day these things are  always disappointing when we were  putting the paris deal together we  jokingly used to say that the thing we  were aiming for was for everybody to be  equally disappointed now at the end of  the day what we've come out of here is a  hard one  agreement out of glasgow i would argue  that with the commitments in place with  the additional step up that gives us a  pathway to get to where we want to be  does it guarantee we'll deliver it  absolutely not is there is the  possibility of failure still on the  table of course it's still there do  china and india and every other country  need to step up further and go further  yes absolutely they do but what we've  seen i would say is some elements of  this agreement that are significant i  mean we're now talking about 1.5 as a  threshold we're now talking about  renewed finance for adaptation new  commitments from national countries some  transformative commitments on forests  and methane and coal  some of those have gaps in them and of  course you can chuck rocks is it enough  absolutely not will i take it as a step  in a process that unfolds over multiple  years to get us to where we need to go  that's inevitably going to be slower  than we would want to  i would say actually it does take us  forward okay as i let's talk about some  of those things then um some of the the  the more positive things that came out  of of glasgow tom mentioned  deforestation there uh for example more  than a hundred countries have pledged  pledged to end deforestation by 2030. a  similar pledge was made in 2014 of  course nothing came of that surely this  time something really can fundamentally  happen  well i want to start by saying that i  don't want to come across as doom and  gloom i am typically doom and bloom  but i do want to say that this new york  deforestation pledge that came out uh  seven years ago what did it result in  well there was a 2019 study that  actually looked at the results and it  found that ever since that pledge we've  seen a 41  increase in deforestation our  deforestation rates have increased to  the equivalent of one united kingdom  every single year so pledges are not  performance and when we look at kovid  for instance we started seeing that  people were wearing masks restaurants  change their hours or their opening  behaviors or what have you things  physically change if we're actually  looking at deforestation we would need  to see robust measures for example  whether it is let's say publishing  companies not using trees instead using  ebooks instead of using toilet paper  people starting to use bidets instead of  for example the old growth forest being  logged in places like british columbia  instead of using bioenergy and forest  for fuel all of these things would  actually change and they would be on the  table if we were having a real  discussion another thing that's really  critical to keep in mind is in these  discussions of language that we've been  having they're incredibly incredibly  weak i wanted to picture for example if  we had had 26 years of conferences on  lung cancer and we were not able to use  the word cigarette that is exactly what  is happening here 26 years of cough and  we cannot use the words oil and gas  something is very wrong here i would use  the f word and the f word is feckless  okay it's a good word i wonder what word  you were going to use then but feckless  i will take  tom i i suppose i suppose one of the  issues here is that really if we're  going to if we're going to do this we're  going to do it quickly enough we need  systemic change we need a huge amount of  effort from everybody right around the  world and we've done a a poll today  which says that 40 percent of uk people  support raising taxes to reduce carbon  emissions only 40 percent now that's not  going to be enough is it it's down to  you it's down to me to to make changes  surely in order to reach these  commitments otherwise cop 26 just does  become a talking shop  well i i thought that was a very  interesting statistic when you guys put  it out that 40 would support raising  taxes i mean i agree that that's an  interesting number i wonder how you ask  that question is going to lead to a  different outcome i think actually what  we need to do is shift the tax base so  that we stop taxing things that we want  in our economy you know like employment  and we stop taxing things um that we  don't want like pollution and actually  that shifting can be revenue neutral so  i think there's a lot in how you ask the  question but the the core of what you  described there is actually that we need  systemic change now let's just go back  to the previous point you made because  it's a really great example in 2014 a  group of countries came together and  made this new york declaration on forest  that everybody was very enthusiastic  about but the truth is it was kind of  thin right it was a statement from  countries there was no implementation  plan and so as a result inevitably in  glasgow when us when a similar looking  type of commitment comes out everybody  says you know this is meaningless you  said this seven years ago you didn't do  it it went up by 41  how come you're lying but actually if  you dig under the skin of this  commitment not only is it 100 countries  committing to reversing deforestation by  2030 it's also real money on the table  to the tune of 20 billion i'm so sorry  we are out of time but it's been great  listening to your thoughts tom rivett  karnick and xiatong thank you both very  much
 

